1388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 513 0 R
/E (Predicting the best of 64 beams from sensors works, but GPS does nearly all the work. On the 2-sequence test set, GPS alone reaches 55.6% Top-1 and K90 = 5, meaning 5 beams out of 64 cover 90% of cases, a 92% reduction in sweep. The night-time camera is a weak standalone sensor. Its best model \(CAM-E3\) gets 38.4% Top-1 and K90 = 10.3, only just above the 36.6% majority-beam baseline on Top-1, while LiDAR \(LID-E3\) is at the baseline level \(K90 36.7 vs 34\). Late fusion shows where the sensors help. GPS+camera is the only combination that beats GPS, with K90 4.33 instead of 5, because the camera is right on some frames where GPS is wrong \(error correlation 0.44\). LiDAR adds nothing to GPS. The gain is under one beam on a small test set, so it is a tendency and not a proven improvement. Two bonus studies show the limits of the approach. Models trained on scenario 33 fall to chance on scenario 31, because the position-to-beam mapping is specific to each site. Fusion also depends on GPS: when GPS fails, K90 rises from 5.3 to 14.7 \(missing\) or 25.3 \(corrupt\), and training the fusion weights under simulated failures only partly recovers it \(17.7\).)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [2]
>>
